  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.6 z²) / 2, drawn at height loss / 2.4 over x, z in [-2.4, 2.4]. x is the steep direction, so plain descent diverges when the learning rate is above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each update: step = −rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian and centred on zero, with spread 0.8 × √((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at b = 64. Noise values come from a fixed seed and keep going in sequence across runs.",
    "Purple arrow: the next mini-batch step (the way the ball moves). Dark arrow: the step the full data set would give. Both are drawn 5 times their true length, one scale for both.",
    "A run ends after 40 updates (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes past 7 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Path points beyond the rim are drawn at the rim, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball makes one update every third host step (10 a second at speed 1), so a single host step may not move it. Changing a setting applies from the next update."
  ]
}